# Story: a developer keeps the day's arrangements as lines of text. One line sets up for
# coding, with chat left floating; a slip of the keyboard changes nothing; a second line, for
# reviewing, opens the mail app, which takes its place when its window appears.
# Spec: F54. Window ids start at 101.
open Xcode
open Simulator
open Terminal
open Slack
layout Xcode:2 | (Simulator / Terminal) | Desktop
expect ok
expect count docked 3
expect left-of 101 102
expect above 102 103
expect floating 104
expect visible 104
expect camera root
zoom 101
expect fills 101
expect hidden 103
# Mixing | and / without brackets is refused, and so is a line that finds nothing.
layout Xcode | Simulator / Terminal
expect fail
layout Xcod | Simulatr
expect fail
expect fills 101
expect count docked 3
# The same line again only looks: the arrangement is already there.
layout Xcode:2 | (Simulator / Terminal) | Desktop
expect ok
expect camera root
back
expect fills 101
# Reviewing: the browser is not open and is skipped, Mail is opened.
layout --open Slack | Desktop | (Mail / Terminal)
expect ok
expect count docked 2
expect left-of 104 103
expect floating 101
expect floating 102
open Mail
expect docked 105
expect above 105 103
expect count docked 3
# The Desktop alone: back to a normal Mac on this display.
layout Desktop
expect ok
expect count docked 0
expect visible 101
expect visible 105
# The first line a laptop user types, in a Terminal the line does not mention: three columns leave the usual Desktop too
# narrow to use, so it is made wide enough, and the Terminal stays in view with the keyboard in it.
focus 103
layout Xcode | Simulator | Slack
expect ok
expect count docked 3
expect floating 103
expect visible 103
expect visible 105
# Other sizes for the same windows: the saved view still leads to Xcode.
zoom 101
save-view 1
show-all
layout Xcode:2 | Simulator | Slack
expect ok
view 1
expect ok
expect fills 101
